# The board, and then the things one board can differ in: the CrowPanel's PCB revision and pixel clock, and the
# orientation. Everything else is the module and the panel, which do not vary.
#
# **The board follows the chip.** `build.sh --board <name>` applies `sdkconfig.board.<name>`, which sets
# `CONFIG_IDF_TARGET`, and each board here is offered only on its own chip, so with one board per chip the
# choice is already made. A second board on a chip needs a line in its `sdkconfig.board.` file to pick it.
#
# **The CrowPanel's default revision is the board Elecrow sells now.** V1.3 and later is the default. The
# revisions differ only in the backlight bytes the STC8 is sent, and the firmware cannot
# read the revision back, so a build for the wrong revision is wrong without a sign.

menu "Relay display"

choice RELAY_BOARD
    prompt "Board"
    default RELAY_BOARD_WAVESHARE_P4_10_1 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    default RELAY_BOARD_CROWPANEL_ADV7
    help
        The board the image is for. Its pins, panel and touch controller are in a header of its own
        under `main/boards/`, which `main/config.h` includes, and its panel driver is one file
        (`main/board_panel.h` lists them). Everything else in the firmware is the same on every board.

    config RELAY_BOARD_CROWPANEL_ADV7
        bool "Elecrow CrowPanel Advance 7.0 (ESP32-S3, 800 x 480 RGB)"
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3

    config RELAY_BOARD_WAVESHARE_P4_10_1
        bool "Waveshare ESP32-P4-WIFI6-Touch-LCD-10.1 (ESP32-P4, 800 x 1280 MIPI-DSI)"
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
        help
            Groundwork: not yet compiled to an image, and not yet on a bench (README.md, "Boards").
endchoice

choice RELAY_BOARD_REV
    prompt "Board revision"
    depends on RELAY_BOARD_CROWPANEL_ADV7
    default RELAY_BOARD_ADV_V13
    help
        The CrowPanel Advance 7.0 (DIS02170A) revision the build is for. The two differ in
        one thing the firmware acts on: the bytes the STC8H1K28 microcontroller is sent to
        switch the backlight to full brightness and to off. V1.2 uses different values
        from V1.3, V1.4 and V1.5, which is what Elecrow sells now.

        **V1.2 and V1.3 or later cannot be told apart in software.** The STC8 takes any byte
        it is sent and does not answer with its revision, so nothing here can read it. A
        build for the wrong revision sends bytes its board may not use, and no log says so.
        Choose the board's revision when the image is built, and check the backlight on the
        bench (README.md, "The board").

    config RELAY_BOARD_ADV_V12
        bool "V1.2"

    config RELAY_BOARD_ADV_V13
        bool "V1.3, V1.4 or V1.5"
endchoice

config RELAY_PCLK_HZ
    int "Pixel clock (Hz)"
    depends on RELAY_BOARD_CROWPANEL_ADV7
    range 15000000 50000000
    default 16000000
    help
        The RGB pixel clock. Elecrow's examples for V1.3 and later run 16 MHz in their
        ESP-IDF code, which this firmware follows, and 18 MHz in their PlatformIO project.
        Their V1.2 examples run 21 MHz. The default is the ESP-IDF value.

        At 16 MHz a frame is 820 by 500 clocks, so the panel refreshes about 39 times a
        second. A picture that flickers or breaks up on the bench is the first thing to
        check. README.md lists what to look for and which value to try next.

config RELAY_BOUNCE_LINES
    int "RGB bounce buffer (lines, 0 = off)"
    depends on RELAY_BOARD_CROWPANEL_ADV7
    range 0 48
    default 10
    help
        The RGB driver reads the frame from PSRAM as the panel scans. Wi-Fi, flash and
        the CPU share that bus, and when a read is late the picture shifts sideways
        and stays shifted until the next restart of the panel. A bounce buffer is two
        strips of this many lines in internal RAM: the driver fills them from the frame
        buffer ahead of the scan, so a late PSRAM read costs a strip and not the whole
        picture. 10 lines is Elecrow's own ESP-IDF value for this panel. Each line is
        1600 bytes, twice. The count must divide 480 evenly.

config RELAY_DEV_DIAG
    bool "Development diagnostics (timing and task logs)"
    default n
    help
        **A bench build, never a shipped one.** Logs each setup-page connection's life
        and each request's phases in milliseconds, and every five seconds the heap and
        FreeRTOS's per-task CPU share. `sdkconfig.dev` turns it on.

choice RELAY_ROTATION
    prompt "Panel orientation"
    default RELAY_ROTATION_0
    help
        How the panel is mounted. This is the starting value of the stored rotation
        setting, which the device's own page can change later. For 180
        degrees the driver mirrors in both axes, so the frame is drawn the same way
        in either orientation.

    config RELAY_ROTATION_0
        bool "Upright"

    config RELAY_ROTATION_180
        bool "Upside down (mirrored in x and y)"
endchoice

config RELAY_ROTATION_DEG
    int
    default 180 if RELAY_ROTATION_180
    default 0

config RELAY_CAPTION_SILENCE_S
    int "Seconds of silence before a caption stream is reopened"
    range 3 600
    default 45
    help
        Relay sends a ping on a quiet caption stream every 15 seconds. A stream with
        nothing at all for this long is a connection that died without closing, which a
        Wi-Fi drop leaves behind, and the panel opens it again with ?last_id=. Three
        pings' worth by default. `sdkconfig.qemu` shortens it so the emulator suite can
        see one happen.

config RELAY_UPDATE_CONFIRM_S
    int "Update: seconds a new image has to reach Relay"
    range 30 86400
    default 600
    help
        A new firmware image boots on approval and has this long, from its boot, to check in with Relay and be
        answered with a 200. If it does not, the panel restarts and the bootloader returns it to the image it
        replaced (`update_format.h`). Ten minutes covers a Wi-Fi drop and a slow Relay; a longer value keeps a
        bad image running for longer. `sdkconfig.qemu` shortens it so the emulator suite can see a rollback.

config RELAY_UPDATE_QUIET_S
    int "Update: seconds with no new caption line before the panel restarts"
    range 1 3600
    default 120
    help
        A verified image waits for a pause in the room: the panel restarts when no caption line has arrived for
        this long. The operator's "Update now" skips the wait. `sdkconfig.qemu` shortens it.

config RELAY_QEMU
    bool "Build for qemu-system-xtensa (emulator suite)"
    default n
    help
        **The CrowPanel's only.** `build.sh` refuses `--qemu` for another board. The option itself is
        offered on every chip, because `main/idf_component.yml` reads it to decide whether to fetch the
        virtual panel, and an option that does not exist there stops the configure.

        **A test build, never a shipped one.** It swaps the two things
        qemu-system-xtensa does not model and leaves everything else as the
        board builds it.

        The panel is created with Espressif's virtual RGB panel
        (esp_lcd_qemu_rgb, the managed component) in place of the RGB driver.
        The virtual panel has no mirror, no frame-complete event and no pixel
        clock, so the 180 degree turn is done in software on the way into each
        strip. The backlight calls do nothing, because the emulator has no STC8 to send
        them to, and the boot line still says the backlight is on.
        The frame buffer the virtual panel reads is its own, not PSRAM.

        Wi-Fi is not started. The decision the board would make is logged,
        with a fixed line saying the radio is skipped, because the emulator has
        no radio model.

        Turned on by `sdkconfig.qemu`, which `build.sh --qemu` applies on top of
        `sdkconfig.defaults`. `tests/firmware_compact` reads the image it makes.
        Off by default, and the managed component is only fetched when this is
        on (`main/idf_component.yml`).


# **Three options that are not this firmware's, declared so the ESP32-P4 build can configure.** The component
# manager solves `esp_wifi_remote`'s dependency on `esp_hosted` by reading every esp_hosted release, and the
# newer ones (2.x and 3.x) carry optional USB dependencies behind these three options. The pinned 1.4.7 has no
# such options, so they exist in no Kconfig file, and ESP-IDF 5.4.4 stops the configure on an option a manifest
# names that it cannot find ("Missing required kconfig option after retry"). Declared here as 0, they are
# found (the manifests compare them with 1, so they are numbers). Each has a prompt because the manager reads `sdkconfig.json`, which leaves out an option that is off
# and has none. To be removed when esp_hosted moves to a release that declares them itself.
if IDF_TARGET_ESP32P4
    config ESP_HOSTED_HOST_USB_CDC_ACM_MANIFEST
        int "esp_hosted over USB CDC-ACM (not used; leave at 0)"
        range 0 0
        default 0

    config ESP_HOSTED_HOST_USB_CHERRYUSB_MANIFEST
        int "esp_hosted over CherryUSB (not used; leave at 0)"
        range 0 0
        default 0

    config ESP_HOSTED_HOST_USB_RNDIS_MANIFEST
        int "esp_hosted over USB RNDIS (not used; leave at 0)"
        range 0 0
        default 0
endif

endmenu
